    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d570">

                <pb facs="0601" n="595" sameAs="frus:frus1894app1/pg595"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Adee to Sir Julian Pauncefote.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1893-07-25"><hi rendition="#italic">July 25,
                        1893</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt of your note of the 20th instant, wherein you inform me, by direction of
                    the Earl of Rosebery, that telegraphic instructions were, on the 4th instant,
                    addressed by his lordship to Her Majesty’s consul at Apia to the effect that a
                    British man-of-war would be dispatched to Samoa to cooperate in the
                    reestablishment of good order with the vessels of the two other treaty powers,
                    and that the consuls of the three powers would be consulted and should furnish
                    the fullest information, but that the decision as to what steps should be taken
                    is left entirely to the discretion of the naval commanders.</p>
                <p>The U. S. consular representative at Apia has been advised by telegraph of this
                    action.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Alvey A. Adee</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Acting Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
